1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:01,700
It's Monday, nine a.m.,

2
00:00:01,712 --> 00:00:04,025
and the biggest client of the year has just

3
00:00:04,025 --> 00:00:06,988
called. The proposal they received on Friday had the

4
00:00:06,988 --> 00:00:09,625
wrong price, forty thousand dollars too low.

5
00:00:09,788 --> 00:00:12,275
So Richard Hale walks into the meeting room,

6
00:00:12,550 --> 00:00:14,762
drops the printout on the table and,

7
00:00:14,938 --> 00:00:17,037
before anyone can say a word,

8
00:00:17,262 --> 00:00:18,825
turns straight to Daniel.

9
00:00:18,988 --> 00:00:22,025
"Who sent this without checking?" And here's the funny

10
00:00:22,025 --> 00:00:24,925
part: Richard approved that number himself,

11
00:00:24,925 --> 00:00:26,262
on Friday afternoon,

12
00:00:26,488 --> 00:00:29,650
in an email that's still sitting in Daniel's inbox.

13
00:00:29,812 --> 00:00:32,175
But by the time anyone remembers that,

14
00:00:32,312 --> 00:00:34,788
the whole room is already looking at Daniel.

15
00:00:34,950 --> 00:00:35,975
So tell me,

16
00:00:36,075 --> 00:00:37,450
do you know someone like this?

17
00:00:37,612 --> 00:00:39,200
A boss, a parent,

18
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:40,062
maybe an ex,

19
00:00:40,162 --> 00:00:41,538
someone who is never,

20
00:00:41,538 --> 00:00:45,225
ever wrong, because somehow it's always somebody else's fault?

21
00:00:45,388 --> 00:00:48,088
Well, that person isn't just difficult.

22
00:00:48,250 --> 00:00:50,262
They're listening to a very old voice,

23
00:00:50,262 --> 00:00:52,300
and today we're going to give it a face.

24
00:00:52,914 --> 00:00:53,852
To be fair,

25
00:00:53,852 --> 00:00:54,752
this isn't new.

26
00:00:54,914 --> 00:00:57,302
Anyone who has worked with Richard for more than

27
00:00:57,302 --> 00:00:58,777
a week has seen the pattern.

28
00:00:58,939 --> 00:01:00,576
When the project ran late,

29
00:01:00,576 --> 00:01:01,552
it was the vendor.

30
00:01:01,714 --> 00:01:03,052
When sales dropped,

31
00:01:03,064 --> 00:01:04,139
it was the market.

32
00:01:04,302 --> 00:01:06,226
And when he missed the board meeting,

33
00:01:06,514 --> 00:01:07,739
it was the traffic,

34
00:01:07,814 --> 00:01:09,064
his assistant and,

35
00:01:09,089 --> 00:01:10,602
somehow, the printer.

36
00:01:10,764 --> 00:01:12,776
Yet whenever something goes well,

37
00:01:12,852 --> 00:01:14,776
the story changes completely,

38
00:01:14,864 --> 00:01:16,902
because suddenly it was his strategy,

39
00:01:17,164 --> 00:01:20,202
his call and his twenty years of experience.

40
00:01:20,364 --> 00:01:22,376
Margaret even has a name for it,

41
00:01:22,376 --> 00:01:24,602
which she only uses in the break room.

42
00:01:24,764 --> 00:01:26,226
"Richard's weather report:

43
00:01:26,439 --> 00:01:27,964
sunny when he's in charge,

44
00:01:27,964 --> 00:01:31,258
stormy when he isn't." Back at his desk,

45
00:01:31,308 --> 00:01:33,258
Daniel opens Friday's email,

46
00:01:33,420 --> 00:01:34,321
and there it is:

47
00:01:34,546 --> 00:01:37,020
Richard's reply, four words long,

48
00:01:37,208 --> 00:01:40,120
approving the exact price that's now causing all the

49
00:01:40,120 --> 00:01:42,370
trouble. Now Daniel has a choice,

50
00:01:42,370 --> 00:01:43,946
and neither option feels good.

51
00:01:44,108 --> 00:01:45,908
If he forwards that email,

52
00:01:46,146 --> 00:01:49,108
he's basically calling his boss a liar in front

53
00:01:49,108 --> 00:01:51,546
of everyone. But if he stays quiet,

54
00:01:51,783 --> 00:01:53,883
the mistake becomes his forever.

55
00:01:54,046 --> 00:01:55,870
So he does what most of us do with

56
00:01:55,870 --> 00:01:56,870
a boss like this.

57
00:01:57,033 --> 00:01:58,183
He swallows it,

58
00:01:58,346 --> 00:02:02,170
apologizes, and stays until midnight fixing the proposal,

59
00:02:02,370 --> 00:02:04,433
while a part of him keeps asking how the

60
00:02:04,433 --> 00:02:06,496
person who made the call ended up being the

61
00:02:06,496 --> 00:02:07,470
one who's angry.

62
00:02:08,086 --> 00:02:10,136
At home, his phone rings.

63
00:02:10,298 --> 00:02:11,361
It's his dad,

64
00:02:11,498 --> 00:02:14,336
who got lost driving to his own anniversary dinner,

65
00:02:14,436 --> 00:02:16,098
and who is furious.

66
00:02:16,261 --> 00:02:18,761
Apparently it was the GPS's fault,

67
00:02:18,936 --> 00:02:19,911
and the waiter's,

68
00:02:19,911 --> 00:02:21,548
and also Daniel's mom's,

69
00:02:21,636 --> 00:02:24,373
because she should have reminded him to leave earlier.

70
00:02:24,536 --> 00:02:27,023
And Daniel, for the first time all day,

71
00:02:27,023 --> 00:02:28,123
laughs out loud.

72
00:02:28,286 --> 00:02:31,474
Because suddenly he realizes that this isn't really about

73
00:02:31,474 --> 00:02:33,898
his boss. He grew up with a Richard.

74
00:02:34,061 --> 00:02:35,948
And maybe, if you're watching this,

75
00:02:35,948 --> 00:02:36,736
you did too.

76
00:02:36,898 --> 00:02:40,186
So, what's going on inside people like this?

77
00:02:40,812 --> 00:02:42,187
Let's give it a face.

78
00:02:42,349 --> 00:02:43,412
Meet The Blamer,

79
00:02:43,437 --> 00:02:44,437
the voice that,

80
00:02:44,599 --> 00:02:46,349
the moment something goes wrong,

81
00:02:46,599 --> 00:02:48,787
scans the room for someone to point at,

82
00:02:49,124 --> 00:02:51,449
long before it ever looks in the mirror.

83
00:02:51,612 --> 00:02:54,199
And here's what makes it so frustrating to deal

84
00:02:54,199 --> 00:02:57,024
with. Richard doesn't feel like he's lying.

85
00:02:57,187 --> 00:02:58,637
When The Blamer speaks,

86
00:02:58,774 --> 00:03:01,237
he honestly believes it was Daniel's fault,

87
00:03:01,337 --> 00:03:03,912
because the voice rewrites the story in his head

88
00:03:03,912 --> 00:03:06,362
before he even has time to check the facts.

89
00:03:06,524 --> 00:03:07,637
In other words,

90
00:03:07,637 --> 00:03:09,650
when you argue with a person like this,

91
00:03:09,837 --> 00:03:12,312
you're not arguing with someone who knows the truth

92
00:03:12,312 --> 00:03:13,062
and hides it.

93
00:03:13,224 --> 00:03:15,812
You're arguing with a memory that has already been

94
00:03:15,812 --> 00:03:20,162
edited. Back in 2004 a team of psychologists

95
00:03:20,162 --> 00:03:23,712
led by Amy Mezulis reviewed two hundred and sixty-six

96
00:03:23,712 --> 00:03:25,325
studies, with children,

97
00:03:25,325 --> 00:03:28,162
adults and people from many different cultures.

98
00:03:28,325 --> 00:03:31,087
And they found the same pattern almost everywhere:

99
00:03:31,288 --> 00:03:32,512
when things go well,

100
00:03:32,575 --> 00:03:33,700
we take the credit,

101
00:03:33,700 --> 00:03:35,162
and when they go badly,

102
00:03:35,350 --> 00:03:37,800
we look for something outside of us to blame.

103
00:03:37,962 --> 00:03:40,812
Psychologists call it the self-serving bias.

104
00:03:40,975 --> 00:03:44,000
So yes, The Blamer visits everyone a little.

105
00:03:44,162 --> 00:03:46,512
But in some people it practically moves in,

106
00:03:46,525 --> 00:03:49,062
especially in people under a lot of pressure,

107
00:03:49,288 --> 00:03:51,587
people with a lot of status to lose,

108
00:03:51,625 --> 00:03:54,150
and people who grew up in homes where making

109
00:03:54,150 --> 00:03:55,688
a mistake was never allowed.

110
00:03:56,320 --> 00:03:58,295
So how do you spot The Blamer in someone

111
00:03:58,295 --> 00:04:01,245
you know? Four signs show up again and again.

112
00:04:01,408 --> 00:04:02,982
They apologize with a but,

113
00:04:02,982 --> 00:04:03,945
as in sorry,

114
00:04:03,945 --> 00:04:05,582
but if you had told me earlier.

115
00:04:05,745 --> 00:04:07,982
Their stories always have a villain,

116
00:04:08,032 --> 00:04:09,245
and it's never them.

117
00:04:09,408 --> 00:04:12,008
They remember your mistakes in perfect detail,

118
00:04:12,120 --> 00:04:14,008
and somehow forget their own.

119
00:04:14,170 --> 00:04:16,032
And when you finally bring proof,

120
00:04:16,058 --> 00:04:17,558
they don't change their mind.

121
00:04:17,720 --> 00:04:19,195
They change the subject.

122
00:04:19,358 --> 00:04:21,907
If two or more of those just reminded you

123
00:04:21,907 --> 00:04:23,120
of a specific person,

124
00:04:23,282 --> 00:04:24,495
you're not imagining it.

125
00:04:24,658 --> 00:04:27,108
You've simply been living next to a very loud

126
00:04:27,108 --> 00:04:30,318
voice. And the cost of that voice goes far

127
00:04:30,318 --> 00:04:31,756
beyond one bad Monday.

128
00:04:31,918 --> 00:04:33,106
After that meeting,

129
00:04:33,144 --> 00:04:34,781
the whole team changes.

130
00:04:34,944 --> 00:04:38,031
Daniel starts copying everyone on every email,

131
00:04:38,056 --> 00:04:39,218
just in case.

132
00:04:39,381 --> 00:04:42,944
Margaret checks every number twice and saves screenshots of

133
00:04:42,944 --> 00:04:47,031
every approval. Nobody volunteers for anything risky anymore,

134
00:04:47,168 --> 00:04:50,844
because people are spending their energy protecting themselves instead

135
00:04:50,844 --> 00:04:51,731
of doing the work.

136
00:04:51,894 --> 00:04:54,206
And Richard? Three weeks later,

137
00:04:54,206 --> 00:04:56,581
another proposal goes out with the wrong price.

138
00:04:56,744 --> 00:04:58,994
Same mistake, different client.

139
00:04:59,156 --> 00:05:00,568
Because the real cause,

140
00:05:00,781 --> 00:05:04,131
a process where one tired person approves prices from

141
00:05:04,131 --> 00:05:05,881
his phone on a Friday afternoon,

142
00:05:06,056 --> 00:05:07,344
was never fixed.

143
00:05:07,506 --> 00:05:09,344
That's the hidden price of The Blamer:

144
00:05:09,531 --> 00:05:12,344
you can't fix a problem you've already decided belongs

145
00:05:12,344 --> 00:05:13,256
to someone else.

146
00:05:13,868 --> 00:05:16,618
Still, The Blamer is not a villain.

147
00:05:16,780 --> 00:05:18,780
In fact, for our ancestors,

148
00:05:18,868 --> 00:05:20,793
it worked more like a bodyguard.

149
00:05:20,956 --> 00:05:22,168
In a small tribe,

150
00:05:22,293 --> 00:05:24,330
your reputation was your survival.

151
00:05:24,493 --> 00:05:26,493
If the group saw you as the one who

152
00:05:26,493 --> 00:05:27,318
lost the food,

153
00:05:27,530 --> 00:05:29,943
broke the spear or scared off the hunt,

154
00:05:30,130 --> 00:05:31,368
you could lose your place,

155
00:05:31,368 --> 00:05:32,830
and alone on the savanna,

156
00:05:33,056 --> 00:05:34,668
people didn't last long.

157
00:05:34,830 --> 00:05:37,368
So the brain developed a kind of defense lawyer,

158
00:05:37,605 --> 00:05:40,230
one that jumps in the second something goes wrong

159
00:05:40,230 --> 00:05:43,568
and argues your case before anyone else can accuse

160
00:05:43,568 --> 00:05:45,155
you. On top of that,

161
00:05:45,293 --> 00:05:48,030
feeling capable kept our ancestors trying.

162
00:05:48,193 --> 00:05:51,568
Someone who blamed himself for every failed hunt might

163
00:05:51,568 --> 00:05:55,430
stop hunting, so a little self-protection kept people going.

164
00:05:56,050 --> 00:05:59,050
Today, though, a wrong number in a proposal won't

165
00:05:59,050 --> 00:06:00,787
get you thrown out into the savanna.

166
00:06:00,950 --> 00:06:03,050
But the defense lawyer doesn't know that,

167
00:06:03,188 --> 00:06:05,225
so it reacts as if your whole place in

168
00:06:05,225 --> 00:06:06,400
the group were on trial.

169
00:06:06,562 --> 00:06:08,388
And notice something important:

170
00:06:08,650 --> 00:06:11,925
the more someone's identity depends on looking competent,

171
00:06:12,162 --> 00:06:13,862
the louder this voice gets.

172
00:06:14,025 --> 00:06:16,900
For Richard, who has spent twenty years being the

173
00:06:16,900 --> 00:06:17,987
man with the answers,

174
00:06:18,212 --> 00:06:21,438
admitting one mistake feels like losing everything.

175
00:06:21,600 --> 00:06:23,175
So in a strange way,

176
00:06:23,250 --> 00:06:25,450
the people who blame the most are often the

177
00:06:25,450 --> 00:06:27,312
ones who feel the most fragile.

178
00:06:27,475 --> 00:06:28,675
They're not strong,

179
00:06:28,675 --> 00:06:31,297
they're scared. So that afternoon,

180
00:06:31,522 --> 00:06:33,809
Elena invites Richard for a coffee.

181
00:06:33,972 --> 00:06:36,210
And instead of telling him he was wrong,

182
00:06:36,359 --> 00:06:38,210
which would only wake up the lawyer,

183
00:06:38,460 --> 00:06:40,647
she asks him just one question.

184
00:06:40,809 --> 00:06:42,397
"Forget who for a second.

185
00:06:42,559 --> 00:06:44,109
If nobody were to blame,

186
00:06:44,222 --> 00:06:46,997
what would we fix?" Richard opens his mouth to

187
00:06:46,997 --> 00:06:49,184
say Daniel, and then stops.

188
00:06:49,347 --> 00:06:50,847
Because without a culprit,

189
00:06:50,972 --> 00:06:53,009
the answer is suddenly obvious:

190
00:06:53,247 --> 00:06:55,997
prices go out with only one person checking,

191
00:06:56,122 --> 00:06:57,422
and that person was him,

192
00:06:57,622 --> 00:07:00,472
approving them from his phone on a Friday afternoon.

193
00:07:00,634 --> 00:07:02,284
And so, quietly,

194
00:07:02,284 --> 00:07:03,059
he says it.

195
00:07:03,222 --> 00:07:06,362
"I signed off on that number." And that's the

196
00:07:06,362 --> 00:07:09,237
update. You can't silence someone else's Blamer,

197
00:07:09,237 --> 00:07:10,674
but you can stop feeding it.

198
00:07:10,837 --> 00:07:12,162
Here are four ways.

199
00:07:12,324 --> 00:07:14,062
First, lower the threat.

200
00:07:14,224 --> 00:07:16,524
The voice gets louder when it feels cornered,

201
00:07:16,674 --> 00:07:18,874
so never try to prove a Blamer wrong in

202
00:07:18,874 --> 00:07:19,987
front of an audience.

203
00:07:20,150 --> 00:07:22,062
Second, talk about the what,

204
00:07:22,062 --> 00:07:22,812
not the who.

205
00:07:22,974 --> 00:07:25,274
Ask what happened and how to prevent it,

206
00:07:25,474 --> 00:07:26,899
not whose fault it was,

207
00:07:26,949 --> 00:07:29,024
because once nobody is on trial,

208
00:07:29,124 --> 00:07:31,224
the lawyer has nothing to defend.

209
00:07:31,387 --> 00:07:33,512
Third, bring the facts calmly,

210
00:07:33,512 --> 00:07:35,100
in private and in writing.

211
00:07:35,262 --> 00:07:37,999
Something like: here's Friday's email,

212
00:07:38,112 --> 00:07:40,424
how do we make sure this doesn't happen again?

213
00:07:40,587 --> 00:07:42,912
And fourth, leave them an exit.

214
00:07:43,074 --> 00:07:45,999
People admit far more when admitting doesn't cost them

215
00:07:45,999 --> 00:07:48,137
their dignity. But one more thing.

216
00:07:48,299 --> 00:07:50,299
If a face popped into your head while you

217
00:07:50,299 --> 00:07:51,674
were watching, that's fine.

218
00:07:51,837 --> 00:07:53,674
Just check, once in a while,

219
00:07:53,812 --> 00:07:55,724
whether it's the face in your mirror.

220
00:07:55,887 --> 00:07:57,812
So next time something goes wrong,

221
00:07:57,924 --> 00:07:59,749
notice what your mind does first:

222
00:07:59,912 --> 00:08:01,262
does it look for a fix,

223
00:08:01,262 --> 00:08:02,374
or for a culprit?

224
00:08:03,004 --> 00:08:04,329
At the next team meeting,

225
00:08:04,529 --> 00:08:07,654
Richard does something nobody has ever seen him do.

226
00:08:07,816 --> 00:08:10,092
"That pricing error was partly on me.

227
00:08:10,254 --> 00:08:12,054
I approved it too fast.

228
00:08:12,216 --> 00:08:13,266
From now on,

229
00:08:13,342 --> 00:08:17,079
two people check every proposal." Daniel nearly drops his

230
00:08:17,079 --> 00:08:18,954
coffee, and across the table,

231
00:08:19,116 --> 00:08:21,229
Margaret writes the date in her notebook,

232
00:08:21,379 --> 00:08:23,104
this time for a good reason.

233
00:08:23,266 --> 00:08:24,691
The Blamer is still there,

234
00:08:24,691 --> 00:08:27,229
of course, but smaller and quieter now,

235
00:08:27,366 --> 00:08:30,242
more like a lawyer waiting outside than one running

236
00:08:30,242 --> 00:08:31,729
the trial. So,

237
00:08:31,729 --> 00:08:32,667
a quick recap.

238
00:08:32,829 --> 00:08:35,779
The Blamer makes people point outward the second something

239
00:08:35,779 --> 00:08:37,979
goes wrong. It exists because,

240
00:08:37,979 --> 00:08:39,092
for our ancestors,

241
00:08:39,292 --> 00:08:42,279
a damaged reputation could mean losing the group.

242
00:08:42,442 --> 00:08:44,729
And you deal with it by lowering the threat,

243
00:08:44,942 --> 00:08:46,992
talking about the what instead of the who,

244
00:08:47,229 --> 00:08:50,204
bringing facts calmly and leaving an exit.

245
00:08:50,366 --> 00:08:52,954
Because the people who never admit a mistake aren't

246
00:08:52,954 --> 00:08:54,592
the strongest people in the room.

247
00:08:54,754 --> 00:08:57,367
And the bravest sentence at work is often the

248
00:08:57,367 --> 00:08:59,292
shortest: that one was mine.

249
00:08:59,454 --> 00:09:00,916
So tell me in the comments:

250
00:09:01,154 --> 00:09:03,954
what's the most ridiculous excuse you've ever heard from

251
00:09:03,954 --> 00:09:05,416
a boss, or a parent?

252
00:09:05,579 --> 00:09:08,954
And subscribe, because next time we're meeting Margaret,

253
00:09:09,029 --> 00:09:11,517
and the voice that hates every change.
